-- A bodiless `DECLARE T` is an opaque type, and a bodiless `DECLARE T x y` is
-- an opaque type of arity two. Those two facts together mean the parser cannot
-- tell a parameterised opaque head from a head whose body keyword is misspelt:
-- both are just a sequence of names after `DECLARE`.
--
-- So `DECLARE Conduct IZ STRING` -- a typo for `IS` -- does not fail here. It
-- declares an opaque `Conduct` whose two type parameters happen to be named
-- `IZ` and `STRING`. The mistake still gets caught, but one step later and
-- under a different name: every use site that supplies the wrong number of
-- arguments reports "I expected 2 arguments", which is what this file pins.
--
-- The corollary worth knowing is the case this file cannot show: a misspelt
-- declaration that is never used reports nothing at all.
--
-- See doc/reference/types/DECLARE.md ("Limits") and
-- specs/todo/IMPLICIT-PROPS-DESIGN.md §11.1.1.

DECLARE Conduct IZ STRING

GIVEN c IS A Conduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is antisocial` c IS TRUE
